---
description: Query object conventions (single concern, self.call facade, namespacing, composability)
alwaysApply: true
---
# Query Object Conventions

- One query concern per class. Simple one-line filters stay as model scopes.
- Public entry point is `self.call(...)`, delegating to `new(...).call`.
- Return `ActiveRecord::Relation` for chainability, or `Hash` for aggregations.
- Query objects are read-only. No writes, callbacks, jobs, emails, or side effects.
- Use `preload` when returned records need known associations and no filter/order depends on them.
- Pagination is the caller's responsibility, not the query object's.
- One query object per concern; class name ends with `Query`.
- Receive validated values; never accept raw `params`.
- Expose a class-level facade: `def self.call(...) = new(...).call`.
- Namespace under `DominioX::Queries::Y::`.
- Reuse model named scopes instead of inline `where(...)` when they exist.
- Annotate `self.call` and `#call` with YARD `# @return [ActiveRecord::Relation<X>]`.
- Decompose `#call` into private methods when there are composed clauses.
- Normalize parameter variants in the initializer (e.g., accept `id:` or `ids:` and coerce to a single internal form).
- Accept an optional base relation/scope for composability: `def initialize(scope: Model.all, ...)`.
- Prefer `preload` over `includes` when associations are known and there is no filter or order on the association.

```ruby
# BAD
class RecentOrdersQuery
  def self.call
    new.call
  end

  def call
    Order.where('created_at > ?', 30.days.ago).each(&:touch)
  end
end

# GOOD
class RecentOrdersQuery
  # @return [ActiveRecord::Relation<Order>]
  def self.call(status: nil)
    new(status:).call
  end

  def initialize(status: nil, scope: Order.all)
    @status = status
    @scope = scope
  end

  # @return [ActiveRecord::Relation<Order>]
  def call
    orders = @scope.preload(:customer).where('created_at > ?', 30.days.ago)
    filter_by_status(orders)
  end

  private

  attr_reader :status

  def filter_by_status(orders)
    status ? orders.where(status:) : orders
  end
end
```
